---
// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
//
// "Getting started" — the on-ramp (NOT a fifth reference). It gets you rendering
// in MaxBars (the promoted flagship surface — the only one with the full
// expressiveness), helps you pick a smaller dialect when a constraint calls for it,
// teaches the one discipline that keeps templates simple (shape data in the host),
// and names the one real trap (truthiness) and how to disarm it.
//
// Per the team consensus, this page forks NO normative prose (theory lives in
// concepts.adoc, linked) and adds NO example corpus: every runnable beat reuses an
// already-gated example object, shown through the same mechanism its home gate
// validates — so the page inherits coverage:
//   • maxbars.hello / mustache.truthyZero / classicbars.conditionals / maxbars.conditionals
//       → plain render, gated by scripts/check-tutorial-links.mjs
//   • analyse.zero  → the cross-engine point; its analyze() finding is gated by
//       scripts/check-tutorial-tooling.mjs (here it is a plain ClassicBars render that
//       shows the *opposite* branch from the same data — the trap, made concrete)
//   • flagship      → rendered with the JSONata `transform` applied (OpenInLab's
//       transform prop), gated by scripts/check-jsonata.mjs
import Reference from "../layouts/Reference.astro";
import OpenInLab from "../components/OpenInLab.jsx";
import { examples as mustache } from "../mustache.mjs";
import { examples as classicbars } from "../classicbars.mjs";
import { examples as maxbars } from "../maxbars.mjs";
import { examples as analyse } from "../analyse.mjs";
import { flagship } from "../jsonata.mjs";

const t = (s) => s; // readability helper for inline tag literals (Astro eats bare {{ }})

const groups = [
  {
    label: "Getting started",
    items: [
      { href: "#first-render", label: "Your first template" },
      { href: "#choose", label: "Which dialect?" },
      { href: "#data-prep", label: "Shape data in the host" },
      { href: "#pitfalls", label: "The one trap: truthiness" },
      { href: "#outgrow", label: "When if isn't enough" },
      { href: "#next", label: "Where to go next" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "References",
    items: [
      { href: "/maxbars", label: "MaxBars (flagship)" },
      { href: "/transform", label: "Data shaping" },
      { href: "/analyse", label: "Truthiness" },
    ],
  },
  {
    label: "Play",
    items: [{ href: "/lab/index.html?engine=maxbars", label: "FlatBars Lab ↗" }],
  },
];
---

<Reference
  title="Getting started"
  subtitle="Render your first template in MaxBars — the flagship surface with the full expressiveness — then learn the one habit that keeps templates simple, the one truthiness trap, and when a smaller dialect fits the job."
  groups={groups}
  dialect="maxbars"
  spec="concepts"
>
  <style>
    .choose { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; margin: 0.9rem 0 0.4rem; font-size: 0.94rem; }
    .choose th, .choose td { text-align: left; padding: 0.5rem 0.7rem; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border); vertical-align: top; }
    .choose th { color: var(--fg-faint); font: 600 11px/1 var(--font-mono); letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; }
    .choose td:nth-child(2) { font: 600 13px var(--font-mono); white-space: nowrap; }
    .choose a { color: var(--accent-2); text-decoration: none; }
    .choose a:hover { color: var(--accent); }
    .pair { display: grid; gap: 1rem; margin: 0.9rem 0 1.2rem; }
    @media (min-width: 60rem) { .pair { grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; } }
    .pair .lbl { font: 600 11px/1 var(--font-mono); letter-spacing: 0.04em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--fg-faint); margin-bottom: 0.3rem; }
    .note { margin: 0.9rem 0 1.4rem; padding: 0.7rem 0.9rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--accent); background: var(--accent-softer); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; font-size: 0.92rem; }
    .note b { color: var(--accent-2); }
  </style>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── 1 · First render ─────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="first-render">
    <h2>Your first template</h2>
    <p>
      A template is just text with <code>{t("{{ }}")}</code> holes that data fills in.
      This is <b>MaxBars</b>, the flagship surface. Edit the template or the data below and
      watch it render — through the same engine the FlatBars Lab ships. (The full
      expressiveness — operators, pipes, loop variables — is just below; start simple.)
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={maxbars.hello.template} data={maxbars.hello.data} />
    <p>That's the whole loop: <em>template + data → text</em>. MaxBars is what we recommend by default; next, when you'd reach for a smaller surface instead.</p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── 2 · Which dialect? ───────────────────────── -->
  <section id="choose">
    <h2>Which dialect?</h2>
    <p>
      FlatBars isn't one language — it's a meaning-free core with four surfaces built on
      it. <b>MaxBars is the flagship: the full expressiveness, and what we recommend by
      default.</b> Reach for a smaller surface only when a specific <b>constraint</b> calls
      for it — they aren't tiers of quality, they trade features for guarantees.
    </p>
    <table class="choose">
      <thead><tr><th>Your job</th><th>Use</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td>App views with real expressions (<code>x &gt; 3</code>, pipes, loop vars) — the default</td>
          <td><a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a></td>
          <td>the flagship — infix operators, pipes, bare loop variables; the only surface with the full expressiveness</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Importing existing Handlebars templates</td>
          <td><a href="/classicbars">ClassicBars</a></td>
          <td>Handlebars-faithful (49/50 vs the real engine); your team already knows it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Logic-less email / CMS snippets; Mustache parity</td>
          <td><a href="/minbars">MinBars</a></td>
          <td>the smallest surface — one sitting to learn it all; 184/184 of the Mustache spec</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td>Building your own engine on the core</td>
          <td><a href="/rawbars">RawBars</a></td>
          <td>the austere, meaning-free substrate the others desugar to</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
    <p class="tip">
      It's a superset ladder — RawBars ⊂ ClassicBars ⊂ MaxBars — plus logic-less MinBars off
      to the side. <a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a> gives you everything the others do and
      more, so it's the recommended default; this page's first ten minutes use it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── 3 · Data prep ────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="data-prep">
    <h2>Keep templates dumb: shape data in the host</h2>
    <p>
      The single habit that keeps templates portable and reviewable: <b>do the logic
      where you have a real language</b>, and hand the template a view-model it can walk
      without branching. Here is the whole idea in one cell — a flat list of commits, a
      transform that groups and labels them, and a template with <em>zero</em> branching:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab
      client:visible
      engine={flagship.engine}
      data={flagship.data}
      transform={flagship.transform}
      template={flagship.template}
    />
    <p>
      All the decisions live upstream, as data; the template just iterates. When the
      reshaping gets real, that middle step is <a href="/transform">JSONata</a> — and,
      as the next section shows, it's also how you sidestep the one real trap.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── 4 · Truthiness trap ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="pitfalls">
    <h2>The one trap: truthiness</h2>
    <p>
      <code>false</code> and <code>null</code> are falsy everywhere — portable. The other
      four — <code>0</code>, <code>""</code>, <code>[]</code>, <code>{t("{}")}</code> —
      are <em>not</em>, and engines disagree. In MinBars (and the language-agnostic
      Mustache spec) <code>0</code> is <b>truthy</b>, so a section over <code>count: 0</code>
      <em>renders</em>:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="minbars" template={mustache.truthyZero.template} data={mustache.truthyZero.data} />
    <p>
      Hand the <em>same</em> <code>count: 0</code> to Handlebars (ClassicBars), where
      <code>0</code> is <b>falsy</b>, and the identical question takes the
      <em>opposite</em> branch:
    </p>
    <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={analyse.zero.template} data={analyse.zero.data} />
    <div class="note">
      <b>Same data, opposite output — that's the trap.</b> Don't memorize four falsy
      tables. Two fixes, in order of preference: (1) <b>shape the ambiguity out</b> in the
      host (the previous section) so the template never asks an ambiguous question; (2)
      ask explicitly — <code>{t("{{#if (ne count 0)}}")}</code>. The
      <a href="/analyse">Truthiness analyser</a> renders your template against your real
      data and flags every condition that would flip — with a rule-free fix.
    </div>
    <p class="tip">
      One sharp edge: <code>mustache.js</code> (the JS implementation) treats
      <code>0</code>/<code>""</code> as falsy like Handlebars, <em>not</em> like the spec;
      and MaxBars uses a third rule again (<code>nonEmpty</code>: <code>0</code> truthy,
      but <code>""</code>/<code>[]</code>/<code>{t("{}")}</code> falsy). Which is the real
      reason to lean on data prep, not on truthiness.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── 5 · Outgrow {{#if}} ──────────────────────── -->
  <section id="outgrow">
    <h2>Why the flagship: real expressions</h2>
    <p>
      Here's the expressiveness in one comparison. Coming from Handlebars, a condition is a
      <b>subexpression</b> — a helper application, <code>{t("(gte stock 10)")}</code>
      (ClassicBars, on the left):
    </p>
    <div class="pair">
      <div>
        <div class="lbl">ClassicBars — subexpression</div>
        <OpenInLab client:visible engine="classicbars" template={classicbars.conditionals.template} data={classicbars.conditionals.data} />
      </div>
      <div>
        <div class="lbl">MaxBars — infix</div>
        <OpenInLab client:visible engine="maxbars" template={maxbars.conditionals.template} data={maxbars.conditionals.data} />
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      <a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a> reads it as <code>stock &gt;= 10</code> — with
      <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, pipes (<code>value | helper</code>), and bare loop variables.
      That's the full expressiveness, and why it's the recommended default. It's the same
      engine and prelude as ClassicBars, just an expression surface on top — so if you do start
      on a smaller dialect, you grow into MaxBars without restarting.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- ─────────────────────────── 6 · Next ─────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section id="next">
    <h2>Where to go next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/maxbars">MaxBars</a> — the expressive flagship, tag by tag: infix, pipes, loop variables. <b>Start here.</b></li>
      <li><a href="/transform">Data shaping</a> — the JSONata habit from section 3, in depth.</li>
      <li><a href="/minbars">MinBars</a> — the whole logic-less language, if you need Mustache parity.</li>
      <li><a href="/lint">Linting &amp; migration</a> — keep templates canonical, import Handlebars.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</Reference>
